  "query_preview": "..While an area of high pressure remains centered over\nthe Four Corners region today, the overall pattern will begin to\nshift as an offshore trough in the PacNW region begins to chip\naway at the northern extent of the high. What this means for us\nis the introduction of an overall drier airmass as we head into\nthe middle of the upcoming week. Hot temperatures are expected to\nremain in place through the next 7 days, with slight moderation\nthrough the first half of the week as a push of moisture surges\nahead of the drier airmass. As the drier airmass is introduced, a\nresurgence of hotter temperatures is expected areawide through the\nremainder of the week.\n\nToday, a subtle shortwave embedded within the southwestern portion\nof the high will lift into the southern half of Utah, helping to\npush showers and thunderstorms off terrain features later this\nafternoon. Ahead of this feature, regional mesoanalysis already\nnotes a finger of enhanced PWAT values (1.2+ inches) pushing into\nfar southwest Utah, with a broad 1 inch of PWAT extending to near\nBeaver County and areas east. With the presence of relatively \nhigh PWAT values across southern Utah, anticipate heavy rainfall \nwith any storms that develop. As such, the risk of flash flooding \nover slot canyons, dry washes, slick rock areas, and recent burn \nscars will all remain a possibility through today.\n\nThis moisture will remain in place across southern Utah through\nat least Wednesday, raising at least an isolated concern for heavy\nrainfall over the aforementioned areas each day. This stream of\nmoisture will be pushed farther northward Tuesday and Wednesday,\nbringing concerns of the same hazards across northern Utah.\nThereafter, the trough offshore will remain in place and introduce\na much drier southwest flow across Utah and southwest Wyoming.\nOverall chances for showers and thunderstorms are expected to\ndecrease substantially Thursday forward, with only isolated\nshowers and thunderstorms anticipated to form over a portion of\nUtah's high terrain. The overall risk of heavy rainfall will\ngreatly decrease as this drier airmass spreads into the region. \n\nHot temperatures will also remain a concern through the week\nahead, with minor moderation of heat expected Monday through \nWednesday alongside a resurgence of heat Thursday through\nSaturday. While daytime high temperatures will be upwards of 5-10\ndegrees above average, overnight low temperatures will be the main\nconcern as little cooling is expected to occur, raising the\noverall risk for heat related illness. Along the Wasatch Front,\nand many other areas throughout Utah, overnight low temperatures\nare only expected to fall into the 70s (some areas near 80\ndegrees!) by the minutes just before sunrise. Due to the overall\nlack of recovery alongside hot daytime temperatures, Heat\nAdvisories and Extreme Heat Warnings remain in effect across\nvarious Utah valleys through at least Monday. Discussion on\npotential heat headlines during the second half of the week are\nalready underway.",
